<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Schaffhausen</h1><p class="st-meta">1415 CE – 1798 CE · capital Schaffhausen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rhine city above the great falls that regained its freedom from the Habsburgs in 1415 and joined the Swiss Confederacy in 1501.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Schaffhausen grew around the Benedictine abbey of All Saints, founded in 1049, at the point where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> boats had to unload above the falls. Pledged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1330, it regained imperial status in 1415, when Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iv-duke-of-austria">Frederick IV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was outlawed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>, and afterwards bought out the remaining claims.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> dominated the council from 1411. Schaffhausen lived from moving goods around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> Falls, especially salt, wine and grain. It allied with the Confederates in 1454 and joined as the twelfth canton in 1501. It adopted the Reformed faith in 1529 and governed a rural territory north of the Rhine.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The old regime ended in 1798 with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, and the canton was restored in 1803. It remains a Swiss canton, the only one lying almost entirely north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1415 CE – 1798 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.86%;width:7.62%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1415 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schaffhausen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schaffhausen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1606&amp;focus=canton-of-schaffhausen" aria-label="Canton of Schaffhausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4881 506 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Canton of Schaffhausen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5201 762L5229 694M5201 762L5186 782M5201 762L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5201 762h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5201 762h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schaffhausen. <a href="/atlas?year=1606&amp;focus=canton-of-schaffhausen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
